WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.891
A key ablation examines how much the higher-order
hyperedges actually matter.

00:00:03.971 --> 00:00:07.862
Surprisingly,

00:00:07.942 --> 00:00:11.833
at small perturbation budgets the lower bound
computed with only edges is nearly identical

00:00:11.913 --> 00:00:15.804
to bounds that add degree-three and degree-four
hyperedges,

00:00:15.884 --> 00:00:19.775
even though the graph contains millions of these
higher-order structures.

00:00:19.855 --> 00:00:23.745
For CIFAR-10 at budget three,

00:00:23.825 --> 00:00:27.716
there are roughly three million degree-three and
ten million degree-four hyperedges,

00:00:27.796 --> 00:00:31.687
yet they have no impact on the computed bound.

00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:35.658
This means edge-only bounds are both cheap and
accurate in the practical regime.

00:00:35.738 --> 00:00:39.629
The aggregated binary bound is the fastest to
compute but much looser,

00:00:39.709 --> 00:00:43.600
and scaling up model architecture yields only minor
gains at low budgets.
